cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or these logs, please cite the manuscript below."
title: "Prompted Rule Execution Adds Nothing over a Rule Engine on Machine-Checkable Rules"
abstract: >-
  We test, on a controlled tabular fraud benchmark, whether supplying a large
  language model with an explicit natural-language rulebook improves on
  executing that same rulebook deterministically. In a specification-matched
  comparison on the primary model the design excludes any LLM advantage above
  half a percentage point, and a rule-level decomposition shows that one of the
  four rules carries essentially all of the discriminative signal. When a model
  is required to execute one machine-checkable rule and ignore every other
  signal, it reproduces the engine's verdict on all 400 instances exactly.
